c14ccdee Hidde Beydals <hidde@hhh.computer> git: worktree, Select the removal by the removed entry's mode
`rmFileAndDirsIfEmpty` removed whatever stood at a path unless it was a
nonempty directory, without knowing the mode of the entry it was
removing. Git's `remove_or_warn` picks the removal from that mode [1]:
`rmdir` for a gitlink, `unlink` for everything else. Two shapes
separate them, and both were removed here. An empty directory standing
where a regular entry is tracked is refused by `unlink`, and a file
standing where a gitlink is tracked is refused by `rmdir`. A user who
replaced a submodule worktree with a file therefore lost it to a hard
reset that dropped the gitlink.

Take the mode as an argument and select the removal from it, keeping
the non-recursive behaviour, the leading-symlink refusal, and the rule
that a refused removal keeps the entry, stops the upward walk and
returns nil. A hard or keep reset reads the mode from the stored index
entry it removes, as Git's `unlink_entry` does, before the index is
reset: a file staged where HEAD has a gitlink is unlinked, and a file
standing where the index has a gitlink is kept. A path the index lacks
gets `unknownMode`. The modes are read into a map once per reset, as a
lookup per removal in the index would scan all its entries. The SkipWorktree pass reads the mode from the index
entry it already holds.

Merge resets still pass `unknownMode`, which keeps only the shape both
removals refuse, a nonempty directory, as before. They refuse a file or
directory standing at a tracked path with `ErrUnstagedChanges` before
removing anything, so the two shapes never reach them.

Tests cover both shapes against the helper on memfs and osfs, and
through a hard reset of a staged entry; the hard reset tests fail
when the index entry's mode is ignored.

[1]: https://github.com/git/git/blob/v2.54.0/entry.c#L595-L613

